1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,333
DappGo existed before this hackathon.

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:06,300
Everything here was built
from October 27 to November 4.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:09,733
A finance post goes viral.

4
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:11,466
Thousands repost it.

5
00:00:11,900 --> 00:00:12,533
Is it right?

6
00:00:12,933 --> 00:00:15,700
This one has a real number
— and the wrong period.

7
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,466
Numbers like this move money,
and wrong numbers lose it.

8
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:21,466
And it's not just text.

9
00:00:22,200 --> 00:00:23,000
Screenshots,

10
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:24,233
PDFs,

11
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:25,300
podcasts,

12
00:00:25,466 --> 00:00:27,133
videos — numbers everywhere.

13
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,133
How do you check all of them?

14
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,833
Asking a model directly
gets one in five right.

15
00:00:35,500 --> 00:00:39,000
Give the same model SEC evidence,
and let a program decide:

16
00:00:39,166 --> 00:00:40,366
nine in ten —

17
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,966
on held-out claims, 240 of 266.

18
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:50,200
Meta's Muse Spark extracts each
claim and picks the SEC lookup.

19
00:00:51,200 --> 00:00:53,800
Python does the arithmetic
and returns the verdict.

20
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:57,266
Model proposes; program decides.

21
00:00:58,200 --> 00:00:59,700
Live, on the site.

22
00:01:01,333 --> 00:01:02,266
Paste the post.

23
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,266
Muse Spark calls the SEC tools.

24
00:01:08,466 --> 00:01:09,333
Four claims.

25
00:01:09,500 --> 00:01:11,233
Four SEC lookups.

26
00:01:11,400 --> 00:01:13,866
Each filed number comes
back with its evidence ID.

27
00:01:14,800 --> 00:01:16,800
The clock is the model's thinking time.

28
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:20,133
Then the program decides.

29
00:01:23,500 --> 00:01:25,100
Contradicted, with the filing.

30
00:01:26,266 --> 00:01:27,833
And here is the corrected draft.

31
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:35,866
Apple's annual revenue was $416.2
billion; the quarter was $102.5 billion.

32
00:01:39,966 --> 00:01:42,200
Every SEC call is on the record.

33
00:01:43,300 --> 00:01:44,533
You can see each call.

34
00:01:48,533 --> 00:01:49,566
The program's verdict.

35
00:01:51,166 --> 00:01:52,566
Cost and time, on the receipt.

36
00:01:56,200 --> 00:01:59,533
The same checks run in batch
from the command line — text,

37
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:03,066
screenshots through Muse OCR,

38
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:06,133
audio through Muse Voice Transcribe.

39
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:10,633
On saved Threads posts,
the median was 6.8 seconds.

40
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:15,733
At that median, a thousand posts
take about two hours on one worker.

41
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:20,733
Ninety point two percent
on held-out claims.

42
00:02:21,700 --> 00:02:24,466
It wrongly says 'supported' once in 139.

43
00:02:25,900 --> 00:02:27,566
Two cents and ten seconds a claim.

44
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:32,166
On real posts, coverage is
reported separately from accuracy.

45
00:02:34,200 --> 00:02:36,400
Every number, checked against the filing.

46
00:02:37,100 --> 00:02:39,266
hackathon dot dappgo dot com.

47
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:52,433
Built with Meta Muse Spark, on Baseten.

48
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:54,066
DappGo.

